{{smaller|the forehead and hindhead. See ''calvarium'', ''sinciput'', and cuts under ''bird''<sup>1</sup>, ''brain'', ''cranium'', and ''skull''<sup>1</sup>. (''b'') The summit or top of a hill, or the like. ''Derham.'' (''c'') The point of the heavens directly overhead; the zenith.}}

'''2.''' In ''math.'', a point of a figure most distant from the center; any convex angle of a polygon. {{smaller|—'''Principal vertex of a conic section,''' the point where the transverse axis meets the curve.—'''Vertex of an angle,''' the point in which the two lines meet to form the angle.—'''Vertex presentation, vertex delivery.''' See ''presentation''<sup>1</sup>, 6.}}

'''vertical''' (vėr′ti-kal), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< F. ''vertical'' = Sp. Pg. ''vertical'' = It. ''verticale'', < ML. *''verticalis'', < L. ''vertex'' (''-tic-''), the highest point, vertex: see ''vertex''. Cf. ''vortical''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Of or relating to the vertex; situated at the vertex, apex, or highest point; placed in the zenith, or point in the heavens directly overhead; figuratively, occupying the highest place.
{{smaller block|I behold him [Essex] in his high-noon, when he . . . was ''vertical'' in the esteem of the soldiery.
{{right|''Fuller'', Worthies, Herefordshire, II. 77.}}
If zeal . . . be short, sudden, and transient, . . . it is to be suspected for passion and frowardness, rather than the ''vertical'' point of love. ''Jer. Taylor'', Holy Living, iv. 3.
<poem>’Tis raging noon; and, ''vertical'', the sun
Darts on the head direct his forceful rays.</poem>
{{right|''Thomson'', Summer, l. 432.}}}}

'''2.''' Specifically, being in a position or direction perpendicular to the plane of the horizon; upright; plumb. {{smaller|A vertical line or plane is one in which, if produced, the vertex or zenith lies. The word is applied to a number of tools and machines, to indicate the position in which they are placed or used: as, the ''vertical'' mill; a ''vertical'' planer.}}

